<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define randomization.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Distinguish random sampling from random assignment.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define pseudoreplication.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a completely randomized design.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a randomized complete block design.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a factorial experiment.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define interaction.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A study randomly assigns <m>60</m> subjects equally to three treatments.
How many independent experimental units receive each treatment?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Four classrooms are randomized to two teaching methods, and <m>25</m>
students are measured in each classroom. Identify the experimental and
observational units.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why the previous experiment does not have <m>100</m> independently
randomized experimental units.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A <m>2^3</m> factorial design is planned. How many treatment combinations
are required?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A <m>2^5</m> full factorial design is planned. How many treatment
combinations are required?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A factor has levels coded <m>-1</m> and <m>+1</m>. If <m>A=-1</m> and <m>B=+1</m>,
find the coded value of the <m>AB</m> interaction.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A <m>3\times4</m> factorial design has <m>5</m> independent replicates per
treatment combination. Find the total sample size.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A cluster-randomized experiment has average cluster size <m>m=20</m> and
intraclass correlation <m>\rho=0.05</m>. Compute the approximate design
effect.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Using the previous design effect, find the approximate effective sample
size if <m>n=400</m> individuals are observed.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Construct a simple <m>3\times3</m> Latin square using treatments <m>A,B,C</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why a before-and-after study on the same individuals is not an
independent-groups design.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain how randomization protects against systematic treatment-group
differences.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why randomization does not guarantee exact balance in every
experiment.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain mathematically why positive within-pair correlation can improve
the precision of a matched-pairs design.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why blocking can reduce residual variance.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that a full factorial experiment with factors having
<m>a_1,\ldots,a_k</m> levels requires</p>

<p>
<md>\prod_{j=1}^{k}a_j</md>
</p>

<p>treatment combinations.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Construct the complete design table for a <m>2^3</m> factorial using
<m>-1,+1</m> coding.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Add columns for</p>

<p>
<md>AB,\ AC,\ BC,\ ABC</md>
</p>

<p>to the <m>2^3</m> design.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the main-effect columns in a balanced <m>2^k</m> factorial are
orthogonal.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why factorial designs can reveal interactions that one-factor-at-
a-time experiments cannot.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain the distinction between crossed and nested factors.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Give an example of a split-plot experiment and identify the whole-plot
and subplot factors.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why split-plot effects use different error terms.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why repeated measurements from the same person are correlated.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain the purpose of counterbalancing treatment order.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain how carryover effects can bias a crossover experiment.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why cluster randomization usually requires more total
observations than individual randomization for the same power.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain the distinction between internal and external validity.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why treatment noncompliance can complicate causal interpretation.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why intention-to-treat analysis preserves randomization.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why analyzing only compliant participants may create selection
bias.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why attrition can threaten internal validity.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain the difference between experimental confounding and observational
confounding.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>If a defining relation is</p>

<p>
<md>I=ABC,</md>
</p>

<p>derive the aliases of <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain the difference between Resolution III, IV, and V fractional
factorial designs.</p>
</statement>
